Q: Split kotlin function for immediate and suspend parts A: <p><code>foo</code> can take an existing <code>CoroutineScope</code> that it then uses to launch coroutines. It should not use the <code>coroutineScope</code> function, as that will create a new scope, and <code>foo</code> will only return after all the coroutines in the new scope has completed.</p>
<p>Here I added a <code>CoroutineScope</code> as the extension receiver of <code>foo</code>.</p>
<pre><code>suspend fun CoroutineScope.foo() {
    delay(100)
    a = 2
    launch {
        delay(1000)
        a = 3
    }
}

suspend fun bar() = coroutineScope {
    foo()
    println(a) // 2
}
</code></pre>
<p>Or you can pass the scope as a regular parameter.</p>
<pre><code>suspend fun foo(scope: CoroutineScope) {
    delay(100)
    a = 2
    scope.launch {
        delay(1000)
        a = 3
    }
}

suspend fun bar() = coroutineScope {
    foo(this)
    println(a) // 2
}
</code></pre>

Q: OpenLiberty - how can I set a custom SSLContext for a JAX-WS client? A: <p>Short answer is no.</p>
<p>Unfortunately, there is no Open Liberty or JAX-WS specification API that supports programmatically setting a custom SSL Context on a JAX-WS client.</p>
<p>That said, there is a possible configuration work around in Open Liberty.</p>
<p>You can configure a non-default SSLContext via Open Liberty's SSL configuration in server.xml, you can then associate this non-default SSLContext with a managed JAX-WS client. This would allow you to achieve something similar, but in a non-programmatic way.  You could at least programmatically look up this SSLContext using Open Liberty's JSSEHelper API to obtain this custom SSLContext.</p>
<p>A couple of things to note, the JAX-WS client must be an Open Liberty managed JAX-WS client, i.e. a <code>@WebServiceRef</code> annotated client. You can then use <code>service-ref</code>  configuration in the <a href="https://www.ibm.com/docs/en/was-liberty/core?topic=liberty-ws-bndxml-file#rwlp_jaxws_bnd__ws_port__title__1" rel="nofollow noreferrer">ibm-ws-bnd.xml</a> file to specify the <code>ssl-ref</code> that you wish to associate the JAX-WS client with.</p>
<p>Example custom SSL config in Open Liberty:</p>
<pre><code>    &lt;ssl keyStoreRef=&quot;clientKeyStore&quot; trustStoreRef=&quot;clientTrustStore&quot; id=&quot;customSSLConfig&quot;/&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Example <code>service-ref</code> configuration that sets an <code>ssl-ref</code></p>
<pre><code>  &lt;service-ref name=&quot;service/SayHelloService&quot;&gt;
    &lt;port name=&quot;SayHelloPort&quot; 
       namespace=&quot;http://example.com/&quot;
       ssl-ref=&quot;customSSLConfig&quot;
     /&gt;
  &lt;/service-ref&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Example of using the JSSEHelper API  to programmatically obtain the customSSLConfig SSLContext:</p>
<pre><code>        
import com.ibm.websphere.ssl.JSSEHelper;
import com.ibm.websphere.ssl.SSLException;

...

JSSEHelper jsseHelper = JSSEHelper.getInstance();
try {
    SSLContext sslContext = jsseHelper.getSSLContext(&quot;customSSLConfig&quot;, Collections.emptyMap(), null);}
catch (SSLException e) {

}
</code></pre>
